\chapter{Conditional vs marginal effects, and GEE}

\section{Two different questions}

Take a logistic mixed model for whether patients recover, with a random effect per
hospital. There are two sensible questions you might be asking:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Conditional (subject-specific).} ``For a \emph{given hospital},
        how much does the treatment change the odds of recovery?''
  \item \textbf{Marginal (population-average).} ``Across \emph{all hospitals},
        how much does the treatment change the odds of recovery?''
\end{enumerate}

For a linear model these are the same number. For a logistic model they are
not --- and the difference can be large.

\begin{plainenglish}[Why they differ]
The inverse-logit curve is bent. Averaging probabilities across hospitals and then
converting to odds is not the same as converting each hospital's odds and then
averaging. Whenever the link is non-linear, ``average of the effects'' and ``effect
on the average'' part company.
\end{plainenglish}

\section{Seeing the attenuation}

<<attenuation-sim>>=
set.seed(123)
n_g <- 200; n_per <- 20
g   <- rep(1:n_g, each = n_per)
x   <- rbinom(n_g * n_per, 1, 0.5)
u   <- rep(rnorm(n_g, 0, 2), each = n_per)     # big between-group variation
eta <- -0.5 + 1.5 * x + u                      # TRUE conditional effect = 1.5
y   <- rbinom(length(eta), 1, plogis(eta))
d   <- data.frame(y, x, g = factor(g))

m_cond <- lme4::glmer(y ~ x + (1 | g), family = binomial, data = d)
m_marg <- glm(y ~ x, family = binomial, data = d)
round(c(true_conditional = 1.5,
        glmm_conditional = lme4::fixef(m_cond)["x"],
        glm_marginal     = coef(m_marg)["x"]), 3)
@

The mixed model estimates
\Sexpr{round(lme4::fixef(m_cond)["x"], 2)}, in the region of the true conditional
effect of 1.5. The ordinary logistic regression returns
\Sexpr{round(coef(m_marg)["x"], 2)} --- far below it, and nowhere near the truth we
put in. That gap is not sampling error and it is not bias: the two models are
estimating genuinely different quantities, and only one of them is the number we
simulated.

There is a good approximation for how much the marginal effect shrinks:
\[
\beta_{\text{marginal}} \approx \frac{\beta_{\text{conditional}}}
{\sqrt{1 + 0.346\,\sigma_u^2}}
\]

<<attenuation-formula>>=
sig2 <- as.data.frame(lme4::VarCorr(m_cond))$vcov[1]
round(c(conditional = lme4::fixef(m_cond)["x"],
        predicted_marginal = lme4::fixef(m_cond)["x"] / sqrt(1 + 0.346 * sig2),
        actual_marginal = coef(m_marg)["x"]), 3)
@

<<attenuation-plot, fig.width=7.4, fig.height=4.2, out.width="0.82\\textwidth", fig.cap="Thin grey curves are individual groups, each with the same conditional slope. Their average (the thick orange curve) is visibly flatter than any of them: that flattening is the attenuation of the marginal effect.">>=
par_book()
xs <- seq(-4, 4, length.out = 300)
plot(xs, plogis(xs), type = "n", ylim = c(0, 1), xlab = "linear predictor for x = 0",
     ylab = "probability", main = "Averaging bent curves flattens them")
grid_book()
us <- rnorm(60, 0, 2)
for (u_ in us) lines(xs, plogis(xs + u_), col = adjustcolor(PAL["grey"], 0.5), lwd = 1)
avg <- sapply(xs, function(z) mean(plogis(z + us)))
lines(xs, plogis(xs), lwd = 3, col = PAL["primary"])
lines(xs, avg, lwd = 4, col = PAL["accent"])
legend("topleft", bty = "n", cex = 0.8, lwd = c(1, 3, 4),
       col = c(PAL["grey"], PAL["primary"], PAL["accent"]),
       legend = c("individual groups", "a typical group (u = 0)",
                  "average across groups"))
@

\begin{keyidea}[The exception that saves count models]
For a \textbf{log} link --- Poisson and negative binomial --- the slope
coefficients are the \emph{same} conditionally and marginally. Only the intercept
shifts, by $\sigma_u^2/2$. That is because $\E[e^{u}] = e^{\sigma_u^2/2}$ is a
constant multiplier that the log link absorbs entirely into the intercept. So this
whole headache applies to logistic mixed models, not to Poisson ones.
\end{keyidea}

<<log-link-invariance>>=
set.seed(9)
u2 <- rep(rnorm(300, 0, 0.8), each = 8)
x2 <- rnorm(2400)
y2 <- rpois(2400, exp(0.5 + 0.7 * x2 + u2))
d2 <- data.frame(y2, x2, g2 = factor(rep(1:300, each = 8)))
round(c(true_slope = 0.7,
        glmm  = lme4::fixef(lme4::glmer(y2 ~ x2 + (1 | g2), family = poisson, data = d2))["x2"],
        glm   = coef(glm(y2 ~ x2, family = poisson, data = d2))["x2"]), 3)
@

Both recover 0.7. The slopes agree, exactly as the theory says.

\section{Generalised estimating equations (GEE)}

If the population-average effect is what you want, you can estimate it directly.
GEE models the marginal mean, specifies a \emph{working} correlation structure
within clusters, and then computes robust standard errors that stay valid even if
that working structure is wrong.

<<gee-fit>>=
library(geepack)
data(epil, package = "MASS")
epil$lbase <- log(epil$base / 4)
epil <- epil[order(epil$subject), ]              # GEE requires sorted clusters

gee_ex <- geeglm(y ~ lbase + trt, id = subject, family = poisson,
                 corstr = "exchangeable", data = epil)
summary(gee_ex)
@

<<gee-corstr>>=
gee_ind <- geeglm(y ~ lbase + trt, id = subject, family = poisson,
                  corstr = "independence", data = epil)
gee_ar1 <- geeglm(y ~ lbase + trt, id = subject, family = poisson,
                  corstr = "ar1", data = epil)
round(cbind(
  independence = coef(gee_ind),
  exchangeable = coef(gee_ex),
  ar1          = coef(gee_ar1)
), 4)
round(cbind(
  se_independence = coef(summary(gee_ind))[, 2],
  se_exchangeable = coef(summary(gee_ex))[, 2],
  se_ar1          = coef(summary(gee_ar1))[, 2]
), 4)
@

\begin{center}
\small\sffamily
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.22\textwidth}p{0.40\textwidth}p{0.34\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{\fn{corstr}} & \textbf{Assumes} & \textbf{Use when} \\
\midrule
\fn{"independence"} & no within-cluster correlation & always safe; least efficient \\
\fn{"exchangeable"} & every pair equally correlated & clustering with no natural order (pupils in schools) \\
\fn{"ar1"} & correlation decays with distance in time & evenly spaced repeated measures \\
\fn{"unstructured"} & every pair its own correlation & few time points, many clusters \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Choose between them with the QIC, GEE's analogue of AIC:

<<qic>>=
round(rbind(independence = QIC(gee_ind),
            exchangeable = QIC(gee_ex),
            ar1          = QIC(gee_ar1)), 1)
@

\begin{plainenglish}[Why the working structure matters less than you would think]
The robust ``sandwich'' standard errors are consistent even if the working
correlation is wrong. Getting it approximately right buys efficiency --- narrower
intervals --- not validity. That is GEE's central selling point.
\end{plainenglish}

\section{GLMM or GEE?}

\begin{center}
\small\sffamily
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.26\textwidth}p{0.35\textwidth}p{0.35\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
 & \textbf{GLMM} & \textbf{GEE} \\
\midrule
Estimates & subject-specific (conditional) & population-average (marginal) \\
Random effects & explicitly modelled & not modelled, only adjusted for \\
Gives you $\sigma_u^2$, ICC & yes & no \\
Predict for a specific cluster & yes & no \\
Predict for a new cluster & yes & only the average \\
Handles missing data & MAR & MCAR (unless weighted) \\
Needs many clusters & works with moderate numbers & prefers 40+ \\
Likelihood, AIC & yes & no (QIC instead) \\
Computationally & slower, can fail to converge & fast and robust \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{rulebox}[Which to use]
\textbf{GLMM} when the clusters themselves are of interest --- you want to know how
much hospitals vary, or to predict for a specific hospital, or the clustering is
part of the science. \\
\textbf{GEE} when clusters are a nuisance to be adjusted for, and you want a policy
statement about the population: ``if everybody switched treatment, the average rate
would change by this much.'' \\
\textbf{Either} for Poisson or negative binomial slopes --- they agree.
\end{rulebox}

\section{Getting marginal effects out of a GLMM}

You do not have to abandon the mixed model to get a population-average answer.
Integrate the fitted model over the random-effect distribution:

<<marginal-from-glmm>>=
data(cbpp, package = "lme4")
m_glmm <- lme4::glmer(cbind(incidence, size - incidence) ~ period + (1 | herd),
                      family = binomial, data = cbpp)
sig <- sqrt(as.data.frame(lme4::VarCorr(m_glmm))$vcov[1])
nd  <- data.frame(period = factor(1:4))
eta <- predict(m_glmm, newdata = nd, re.form = NA)      # conditional on u = 0

## Average the probability over the distribution of the random effect
marg <- sapply(eta, function(e)
  integrate(function(u) plogis(e + u) * dnorm(u, 0, sig), -8 * sig, 8 * sig)$value)

round(data.frame(period = 1:4,
                 conditional_u0 = plogis(eta),
                 population_average = marg), 4)
@

The population-average probabilities are pulled towards 0.5 relative to the
typical-herd probabilities, which is the same attenuation seen earlier.

The \pkg{emmeans} package will do this for you, and gives standard errors too:

<<emmeans-marginal>>=
library(emmeans)
emmeans(m_glmm, ~ period, type = "response", bias.adjust = TRUE, sigma = sig)
@

\begin{keyidea}[The three numbers you can report, and what each means]
\textbf{Conditional with $u = 0$:} a herd of exactly average latent risk. \\
\textbf{Population average:} the mean across all herds --- what a policy-maker
wants. \\
\textbf{Conditional on a specific $u_j$:} that particular herd --- what its
veterinarian wants. \\
Say which one you are reporting. Most papers do not, and readers then cannot tell.
\end{keyidea}
